   174	## 5. Multiple Path Options (THE DESIGN DECISION)
   175	
   176	Two orthogonal decisions: **(I) how to determine the bindable queue set**
   177	and **(II) what to do with packets on a stranded queue**. Each fix path is
   178	a (I,II) pairing.
   179	
   180	### Decision I — bindable-queue discovery
   181	
   182	- **I-a: bind-probe at bring-up.** After attaching the shim, attempt one
   183	  throwaway XSK bind on each `rx-N`; the set that succeeds == bindable set.
   184	  Pro: empirically exact, driver-agnostic. Con: extra socket churn at
   185	  startup; the probe bind itself could leave a transient `xsk_pool` that
   186	  EBUSYs the real bind (must close + quiesce — same ZC-teardown timing the
   187	  rebind fix handles).
   188	- **I-b: post-attach topology query.** Query bindable queues from the
   189	  attached-prog topology (e.g. `bpf_xdp_query` channel info / ethtool
   190	  channels after attach). Pro: no extra binds. Con: no kernel API cleanly
   191	  reports "queues that accept an AF_XDP pool"; virtio's reservation isn't
   192	  surfaced as a queryable count — likely unavailable, would still guess.
   193	- **I-c: observed-bound feedback (reactive).** Plan all sysfs queues
   194	  (status quo), but **derive `queue_count` from successfully-BOUND
   195	  bindings**, not registered. On persistent per-queue EBUSY, drop that
   196	  queue from the plan and re-plan (adaptive). Pro: no speculative probe;
   197	  reuses the existing watchdog; converges to the real bindable set. Con:
   198	  one bring-up cycle of partial drop before convergence (mitigated by II).
   199	- **I-d: pin channels first.** `ethtool -L <if> combined N` to `N=workers`
   200	  before binding, so advertised == bound. Pro: deterministic, removes
   201	  surplus at the source. Con: xpf does NO `set_channels` today (new
   202	  netlink/ioctl dependency + privilege); the kernel **refuses** the
   203	  reduction once ZC XSKs exist ("too low for existing zerocopy AF_XDP
   204	  sockets") so it must run *before any bind* and survive rebinds; changes
   205	  RSS width for every driver, not just virtio; risk of fighting the Go
   206	  interface manager.
   207	
   208	### Decision II — stranded-queue packet policy
   209	
   210	- **II-a: black-hole (status quo).** `drop_degraded_transit` → XDP_DROP.
   211	  Rejected — this IS the bug.
   212	- **II-b: redirect to a bound queue.** Make `select_userspace_queue` /
   213	  the missing-binding fallback map a packet on a stranded queue to a
   214	  *ready* binding (e.g. `rx % ready_count` over the bound set, or fall to
   215	  slot 0). Pro: keeps traffic in the AF_XDP firewall fast path. Con:
   216	  **AF_XDP redirect is queue-bound** — `XDP_REDIRECT` into an XSKMAP slot
   217	  bound to a *different* RX queue is dropped by the kernel post-redirect
   218	  (the shim's own comment at `lib.rs:1387` warns of exactly this). So a
   219	  cross-queue redirect to a bound XSK on another queue does NOT deliver.
   220	  This option is **only** viable if the bound XSK shares the packet's
   221	  actual RX queue — which by definition the stranded queue's isn't. ⇒ not
   222	  generally workable for transit on the stranded queue.
   223	- **II-c: XDP_PASS to kernel (degraded fallback).** On a stranded
   224	  (registered-but-not-ready) transit queue, `XDP_PASS` instead of
   225	  `XDP_DROP`, letting the kernel forward (with `ip_forward=1`). Pro: no
   226	  black-hole; the box forwards (unfirewalled on that fraction) instead of
   227	  dropping. Con: the kernel path has no SNAT/policy → asymmetric/leaky
   228	  forwarding; security posture degraded silently unless surfaced. Must be
   229	  a **health-visible** degraded state, and arguably gated behind
   230	  non-strict mode (strict mode keeps DROP, fail-closed).
   231	- **II-d: make queue_count == bound count so no queue is stranded.**
   232	  If `queue_count` counts only BOUND queues, then `rx % queue_count`
   233	  for a packet arriving on an unbound queue `q >= queue_count` maps to a
   234	  *bound* lower queue — but again hits the queue-bound-redirect wall
   235	  (II-b): the lower bound XSK isn't on queue `q`'s ring. So shrinking
   236	  queue_count alone still can't *deliver* a packet that physically arrived
   237	  on an unbound queue; it can only stop *bound* queues from being
   238	  mis-counted. II-d is necessary for correct steering of bound queues but
   239	  insufficient on its own for stranded-queue traffic.
   240	
   241	**The queue-bound-redirect constraint (II-b/II-d wall) is the crux:** a
   242	packet that physically lands on a queue with no bound XSK *cannot* be
   243	delivered into the AF_XDP firewall at all. The only honest choices for
   244	that packet are XDP_DROP (fail-closed) or XDP_PASS (fail-open to kernel).
   245	Therefore the real fix must **prevent queues from being stranded in the
   246	first place** (Decision I) and only use II as a transient/strict-mode
   247	safety net.
   248	
   249	### Recommended path: **I-c (observed-bound feedback) + II-c (PASS in non-strict, DROP in strict) + queue_count-from-bound (II-d for the bound set)**
