   260	  QueueID)+1` (or, more precisely, count of ready bindings), so bound
   261	  queues steer correctly and surplus queues stop inflating the modulo.
   262	- II-c converts the residual black-hole into a kernel-PASS **only in
   263	  non-strict mode** during the convergence window, with a health counter
   264	  + Prometheus gauge + `show` surface so the degradation is never silent;
   265	  strict mode keeps fail-closed DROP. This bounds the blast radius to "a
   266	  few packets PASS to kernel during the first second of bring-up" instead
   267	  of "3/4 of transit black-holed indefinitely".
   268	- I-d (`ethtool -L`) is held as a **contingent Path C** — pursued ONLY if
   269	  Phase 0 shows virtio *structurally* reserves queues that never bind
   270	  regardless of lifecycle (i.e. the bindable set is permanently < sysfs
   271	  count). In that case pinning combined channels to the bindable count at
   272	  bring-up is the cleanest source-fix, and I-c's adaptive drop is the
   273	  fallback. Decision deferred to Phase-0 evidence; not in the committed
   274	  scope.
   275	
   276	## 6. Phased plan
   277	
   278	**Phase 0 — instrumented virtio-MQ repro (MANDATORY FIRST, decides Case 1 vs 2).**
   279	- Venue: the standalone baked-image VM on incus/virtio with **4 combined
   280	  channels** (NOT the loss mlx5 cluster — it can't repro). Confirm
   281	  `ethtool -l ge-0/0/0` shows 4 combined.
   282	- Deploy current master. Capture per-queue: bind outcome (bound vs
   283	  EBUSY-exhausted), `binding.ready`, `userspace_bindings[idx].flags`,
   284	  `userspace_ctrl.queue_count`, and the redirect-stage trace
   285	  (`USERSPACE_TRACE_STAGE_*` with rx_queue/selected_queue/slot) +
   286	  `drop_degraded_transit` reason counters.
   287	- Drive LAN→WAN transit (v4 + v6); record sessions, per-queue drop counts,
   288	  tx_completions.
   289	- **Decision gate:** if all 4 queues bind & forward → **Case 1** (durability-
   290	  only scope + close issue). If a subset EBUSYs / black-holes → **Case 2**
   291	  (full scope). Record which kernel reservation/lifecycle mechanism (attach
   292	  `git show`/dmesg/ethtool refusal) so Path C can be ruled in/out.
   293	
   294	**Phase 1 — durability core (ship in BOTH cases).**
   295	- `queue_count` from BOUND/ready bindings, not registered
   296	  (`queueCountFromBindings` → key on `Ready` or `Bound && XSKRegistered`).
   297	  Regression test: a registered-but-unbound surplus binding does not raise
   298	  `queue_count`.
   299	- Stranded-queue policy II-c: in `lib.rs`, a registered-but-not-ready
   300	  *transit* packet on a queue with no ready binding takes XDP_PASS in
   301	  non-strict mode (kernel forwards) and XDP_DROP in strict mode; add a
   302	  distinct fallback reason + counter (`USERSPACE_FALLBACK_REASON_*`) and a
   303	  Prometheus gauge `xpf_userspace_stranded_queue_*`. Keep the existing
   304	  local/control PASS behaviour. Test: shim unit test asserting
   305	  non-strict→PASS, strict→DROP on not-ready transit.
   306	- Doc: correct `docs/image-validation.md` VENUE WARNING to the post-fix
   307	  behaviour established by Phase 0 + document the degraded-mode semantics
   308	  in `pkg/dataplane/README.md` / a userspace-dp doc.
   309	
   310	**Phase 2 — adaptive queue-drop (ship in Case 2 only).**
   311	- Extend the busy-binding watchdog to track *per-(ifindex,queue)*
   312	  persistent EBUSY across K rebind cycles; on persistence, drop that queue
   313	  from the candidate plan and re-plan with the reduced `queue_count` so the
   314	  shim steers only over bindable queues (closes the stranded-queue window
   315	  for good). Regression test: a queue that EBUSYs K times is removed from
   316	  the plan and the remaining queues forward.
   317	- Interaction guard: the global-`min` planner (`helpers.rs:759`) means a
   318	  single-queue fabric parent already clamps every interface to 1 queue; any
   319	  per-interface queue-drop must NOT resurrect the per-interface-vs-uniform
   320	  planning collision the v6 plan scoped out (#1921 plan history). Keep
   321	  uniform planning; the adaptive drop reduces the *global* bindable count,
   322	  not per-interface asymmetric counts. This sub-decision gets its own
   323	  review pass at /engineer time.
   324	
   325	**Phase 3 — CONTINGENT Path C (ethtool -L), Case 2 + structural-reservation only.**
   326	- Only if Phase 0 proves the bindable set is permanently < sysfs count
   327	  regardless of socket lifecycle. Pin `combined = bindable_count` before
   328	  any bind, survive rebinds, coordinate with the Go interface manager.
   329	  Separate review round; explicitly NOT in the committed PR.
   330	
   331	## 7. Acceptance criteria
   332	
   333	- **Phase 0**: documented per-queue bind+forward matrix on 4-channel
   334	  virtio establishing Case 1 or Case 2, with the kernel mechanism named.
   335	- **No silent black-hole**: a forced partial-bind (test harness marks one
   336	  binding not-ready) yields PASS-to-kernel (non-strict) or DROP (strict)
   337	  with a non-zero health counter — never a silent transit loss with
   338	  `enabled=true`.
   339	- **Bound-aware steering**: `queue_count` equals deliverable-queue count;
   340	  unit test proves a registered-but-unbound surplus binding does not
   341	  inflate it.
   342	- **Live forwarding**: on the 4-channel virtio venue, LAN→WAN v4+v6 transit
   343	  yields non-zero sessions + tx_completions across the bound queue set
   344	  (RX-delivery proof, not "all planned ready"), measured per the #1928
   345	  signal discipline (sessions + tx_completions_total, NOT rx_packets_total).
   346	- **No regression**: mlx5 loss cluster `make test-failover` 14/14 and
   347	  line-rate smoke unchanged (the change is a no-op when all queues bind).
   348	
   349	## 8. Risks
   350	
   351	- **R1 — Case 1 (already fixed).** Static evidence can't rule this out; the
   352	  whole full-fix scope may be unnecessary. Mitigated by Phase 0 as the
   353	  first, cheap step; durability core still worth shipping.
   354	- **R2 — II-c fail-open security posture.** PASS-to-kernel forwards
   355	  unfirewalled on the stranded fraction. Mitigated: non-strict only,
   356	  health-visible, transient (Phase 2 closes the window); strict mode stays
   357	  fail-closed.
   358	- **R3 — queue-bound redirect wall.** Tempting "redirect to a bound queue"
   359	  (II-b/II-d) does NOT deliver (kernel drops cross-queue XSK redirect).
   360	  The plan explicitly avoids relying on it.
   361	- **R4 — adaptive-drop vs uniform planner collision** (Phase 2). The
   362	  global-min planner + per-interface queue-drop can interact badly; scoped
   363	  to a dedicated /engineer review round.
   364	- **R5 — Path C `ethtool -L`** introduces a new privileged netlink
   365	  dependency + RSS-width side effects + ZC-pin ordering; quarantined to
   366	  contingent Phase 3.
   367	- **R6 — venue.** Only the standalone virtio VM repros; the loss cluster
   368	  cannot. Engineer-phase must bake/boot the virtio venue, not smoke on
   369	  loss for the forwarding proof (loss only for the no-regression gate).
   370	
   371	## 9. Test / validation plan
   372	
   373	- Rust shim unit tests: not-ready transit → PASS(non-strict)/DROP(strict)
   374	  with the right counter; `queue_count` from bound set.
   375	- Go unit tests: `queueCountFromBindings` ignores registered-but-unbound;
   376	  watchdog per-queue persistent-EBUSY drop (Phase 2).
   377	- Live (engineer-phase): Phase-0 matrix; 4-channel virtio LAN→WAN v4+v6
   378	  forwarding proof; mlx5 `make test-failover` 14/14 + smoke no-regression.
   379	
   380	## 10. Rollback
   381	
   382	Pure additive guards + a counted policy branch; revert is a single PR
   383	revert. `queue_count`-from-bound is behaviourally identical when all queues
   384	bind (the all-bind case). No schema/wire changes beyond new counter enums
   385	(append-only).
   386	
   387	## 11. Open questions (for reviewers)
   388	
   389	- OQ1: Is Phase 0 Case 1 or Case 2? (Empirical — engineer-phase resolves;
   390	  research can only design for both.)
   391	- OQ2: Should II-c PASS-to-kernel be allowed at all, or is strict-DROP the
   392	  only acceptable stranded-queue policy (i.e. forbid silent unfirewalled
   393	  forwarding even transiently)? This is the central security/availability
   394	  tradeoff the user should rule on.
   395	- OQ3: If Case 2 is structural, is Path C (`ethtool -L combined N`) the
   396	  preferred source-fix over I-c adaptive drop, accepting the new ethtool
   397	  dependency?
   398	- OQ4: Does `queue_count`-from-bound interact with the heartbeat/READY
   399	  bootstrap deadlock the `:207-209` comment warns about (ctrl=0 → no RX →
   400	  not ready)? Need to confirm Bound (not Ready) is the right key so a queue
   401	  that bound but hasn't seen RX still counts.

