docs/research/1888-wg-timers/codex-plan-r3.md:18:   Evidence: v5 stores `last_send_data_ns` on every successful non-empty encap at [plan.md](/home/ps/git/bpfrx/.claude/worktrees/1888-research/docs/research/1888-wg-timers/plan.md:177) and [plan.md](/home/ps/git/bpfrx/.claude/worktrees/1888-research/docs/research/1888-wg-timers/plan.md:335). Linux arms the new-handshake timer only if it is not already pending, then clears it only on authenticated receive: [timers.c](/home/ps/git/linux/drivers/net/wireguard/timers.c:147) and [timers.c](/home/ps/git/linux/drivers/net/wireguard/timers.c:176). With v5, outbound packets every second and zero inbound keep refreshing the stamp, so T7 never reaches 15s; responder-role sessions then wait until T3 instead of repairing the blackhole at T7.
