   249	### Recommended path: **I-c (observed-bound feedback) + II-c (PASS in non-strict, DROP in strict) + queue_count-from-bound (II-d for the bound set)**
   250	
   251	Rationale:
   252	- I-c needs no speculative probe (avoids I-a's self-EBUSY hazard) and no
   253	  new `ethtool -L` dependency (avoids I-d's privilege + ZC-pin + RSS-width
   254	  hazards). It reuses the existing busy-binding watchdog
   255	  (`maps_sync.go:1288`) which already detects `registeredArmed>0 &&
   256	  bound==0` and rebinds — we extend it to *per-queue persistent EBUSY*
   257	  ("this queue never binds across K rebind cycles → drop it from the plan")
   258	  rather than only the all-queues-wedged case.
   259	- `queue_count` switches from `max(registered QueueID)+1` to `max(BOUND
   260	  QueueID)+1` (or, more precisely, count of ready bindings), so bound
   261	  queues steer correctly and surplus queues stop inflating the modulo.
   262	- II-c converts the residual black-hole into a kernel-PASS **only in
   263	  non-strict mode** during the convergence window, with a health counter
   264	  + Prometheus gauge + `show` surface so the degradation is never silent;
   265	  strict mode keeps fail-closed DROP. This bounds the blast radius to "a
   266	  few packets PASS to kernel during the first second of bring-up" instead
   267	  of "3/4 of transit black-holed indefinitely".
   268	- I-d (`ethtool -L`) is held as a **contingent Path C** — pursued ONLY if
   269	  Phase 0 shows virtio *structurally* reserves queues that never bind
   270	  regardless of lifecycle (i.e. the bindable set is permanently < sysfs
   271	  count). In that case pinning combined channels to the bindable count at
   272	  bring-up is the cleanest source-fix, and I-c's adaptive drop is the
   273	  fallback. Decision deferred to Phase-0 evidence; not in the committed
